# Sources grouped into paths

Within the component map, record the entry points an actor can drive and group them into paths,
so each distinct flow of input is swept once.

A source is one concrete entry point — one route, event, message or signal handler, timer,
background job, read site, callback — recorded at its definition, not a declaration, and not a trust
class or ingress family: recording "all authenticated requests" as one source hides every path
behind it. Consider network input, stdin or another process's output, files, environment variables,
IPC, arguments, plugins, deserialization, queues, configuration read at startup, CI jobs others can
trigger and reads of state an attacker-driven flow writes; a trusted-looking file or upstream
service is not necessarily a trusted origin. Do not filter sources because they look low impact.

Sweep every file of the components the map says receive external input with one `sources` child
each, which records its sources, each on the existing path its flow matches or on a path of its
own whose first step is its handoff.
{%- if live %}
Each interface of theirs an environment serves gets a child too.
{%- endif %}

```js
{% if live %}{% include "security/scripts/paths.live.js" %}{% else %}{% include "security/scripts/paths.no-live.js" %}{% endif %}
```

Once every child has finished, reconcile each handoff group into the paths it is: only sources
interpreted identically up to the handoff (thin wrappers or aliases of one entry, say) — the
same parsing, the same state touched, the same call into the same component — share one path, so
`merge` each other path of the group into the one you keep, which takes its sources, premises and
place in candidates' actions. Distinct flows, differing in trust or handling before the handoff,
keep their own paths; when in doubt, keep them apart.
Join groups that are one handoff described differently. Then extend each path past its handoff
with `link` `path_step`: each component the handoff's component hands the input to, in order,
with its location and how. Place each unmatched path's handoff yourself. Every source belongs to
a path, and every input has an outcome. Retract a source or path recorded in error, such as an
internal helper taken for an entry point, with `set_status` `retracted`.

Return the path IDs, the inputs that had no entry points, the unserved interfaces, and what you
could not resolve, such as dynamic registration or generated dispatch, also recorded as context
evidence on its component.
An enumerated inventory is not proven exhaustive coverage.
